From: RD03v2 AI-assisted development <ai-assisted@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] ecm: defunct accelerated flows on FIB policy-rule changes

The v2 ECM tree already handles route-table updates, but a policy-rule-only
change made by mwan3 can move a flow to another WAN without a route event.
Defunct accelerated connections of the affected address family so their next
packets are routed using the current policy rules.

The FIB notifier callback is atomic, so it closes the affected frontend
gate once, records the pending family bit, and queues work only after the
initial FIB dump/replay has completed. The worker drains all coalesced rule
changes in a loop and keeps each affected v2 frontend stop gate closed across
passes. It opens the gates only after an empty-pending check serialized under
RTNL with live rule ADD/DEL callbacks. Each pass waits for pre-existing
Netfilter hook readers with `synchronize_net()` before running the
process-context connection defunct routine. The pinned QSDK source's
temporary-stop counters are moved beside the common frontend locks and
updated with a guarded decrement, so stop updates share synchronization with
packet-hook stop-state checks.

Route notifier registration is also made transactional. IPv4 and IPv6 results
are kept separate, the first error is preserved, and each successful
registration is tracked independently. A partial route-notifier failure
unwinds the initialized ECM database and unregisters only the family that
actually registered. FIB notifier registration is mandatory: it happens only
after route-notifier initialization succeeds. If it fails, `ecm_db_init()`
tears down the database and returns the error. QSDK initializes `ecm_db`
before the packet frontends and conntrack notifier, so the enclosing ECM
module initialization aborts before this module can accelerate traffic.

On normal teardown, the FIB notifier is unregistered and its worker drained
before the database is marked terminating or freed. The patch does not
promise preservation of conntrack/NAT sessions across a public-WAN change.

